## Reality check

The app started as an initiative from the engineers, in collaboration with INSO colleagues from the country offices, who had concluded a few things that pointed towards a mobile app:

- SMS was getting more expensive, which put pressure on country office budgets.
- Internet coverage was increasing, and so was smartphone use: the average NGO worker carries one wherever they are, alongside other communication devices if needed.

Those sessions were the only research behind the app, and the engineers had mostly focused on the technical side. I took the project on wanting to do further research, but with those sessions done, a demo already implemented and two other projects in my pipeline, {% mark text="I decided to focus on design principles instead" /%}. So I based the redesign on a heuristic evaluation and testing with country office colleagues, knowing there would be no baseline to compare against.

## Product

**A mobile way to access real-time incident alerts**

INSO provides NGO workers with incident alerts in their regions, traditionally delivered by SMS and email. These channels work for broadcasting but are limited when it comes to organising, filtering and interacting with alerts in real situations. The app brings the alerts into one environment where users can explore incidents on a map, filter what matters, track updates and stay informed on the move.

{% figure src="/media/projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-product-intro-02.mp4" alt="Walk-through of the redesigned app: the alerts list, an alert opening, and the map with its modes." caption="High-level overview of the app structure and key interactions around alerts." /%}

{% figure src="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-product-visuals.jpg" alt="Five app store screens: the alerts list with search, an alert detail, and the Explore, Proximity and Geofence map modes." caption="Key sections and features: the alert list and the different map modes." /%}

## Context

**The app existed, but the experience did not match the users or the product's potential**

I got into this project out of curiosity after hearing the developers talk about a mobile app they were building. When I went to have a sneak peek, my admittedly biased designer brain thought: what is this, and who is it for? I wondered if it was even meant for INSO as I couldn't see any blue (our main brand colour) anywhere, only some brown and beige.

{% figure src="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-former-version.jpg" frame="before" alt="The app when I joined: a dense dark list of alerts in capitals, an alert detail packed with text under a small map, and a map that shows a single alert pin." caption="Before: Visuals showing what the App looked like when I joined the project team." /%}

{% runin text="Friction" /%} The solution had come first and its value was named later, so there was no problem statement to design against. A quick heuristic evaluation of the app became my starting point. After sharing it with the project lead, I asked to take the app on, with the idea of lining up the UI with the brand, not knowing it would grow into a UX redesign. Along the way it felt like a patchwork every now and then.

## Users

**The app serves two groups**

Recipients are NGO field workers and security and operations staff who need fast, clear, reliable information under pressure. Senders are INSO colleagues in the country offices, who collect, validate and broadcast alerts through the Partners Portal on laptops.

**How I built the picture.** I had no direct access to NGO workers before launch, so country office colleagues, who work with partners daily, stood in as proxies. I interviewed them, ran a heuristic evaluation, and tested the prototype remotely in Maze. Remote testing on an unfamiliar prototype was tricky: some participants expected a native app and tapped everywhere, so I briefed them by email and in the intro and gave them scenario tasks. What I looked at was where people were active, and how close their paths came to the intended one for each task. Partners themselves only weighed in after launch, through a survey.

{% figure src="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-users.jpg" alt="Board in three columns: who (NGO field workers, security and operations staff, INSO field monitors), their key needs, and the product and real-world pain points." caption="Breakdown of the different user profiles, their needs and pain points." /%}

**Composing alerts.** A portable way for INSO staff to compose and broadcast alerts was discussed from the start, but we agreed to focus first on the NGO-facing side: receiving alerts and seeing them on a map.

## Login

**Users were asked to act before understanding what the app does**

The app opened on a login form and asked for location access first.

{% pair %}
{% figure src="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-intro-old.jpg" frame="before" size="half" alt="The old first screen: the login form, with the system prompt asking for location access on top of it." caption="Before: Users were asked to enable location tracking before being introduced to the app" /%}
{% figure src="/media/projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-intro-new-animated.mp4" frame="after" size="half" alt="The new pre-login flow: two onboarding screens explain what the app does before the login appears." caption="After: Redesign of pre-login flow featuring 2 onboarding screens" /%}
{% /pair %}

{% runin text="Friction" /%} When I asked country office colleagues whether this would be clear to partners, they felt a welcome email explaining the app would be enough. I disagreed: someone could install the app today and not open it until an alert arrives a month later, by which point the email is forgotten. The explanation had to live inside the app.

I added two onboarding screens to explain the app's purpose and value, kept intentionally short. Location access is now requested only after login, once people know what the app is for. In-app tutorials appear after login, and an FAQ in Settings gives a reminder later.

The login felt like a backend tool. I aligned it with the brand and improved readability. I also fixed the validation: "Invalid email" used to appear as soon as someone tapped a field, which looked like a bug, and errors now show only when relevant. A small illustration (a person, documents, a lock) signals that the space is private. A short note explains who can log in: "To log into this app, your email must be registered with the INSO Partners Portal. Learn how you can become an INSO partner at ngosafety.org/registration." INSO staff get their own link: "Log in as INSO staff."

{% figure src="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-login-comparison.jpg" frame="compare" alt="Login before: a plain form showing red “Invalid email” and “Invalid password” errors before anything is typed. After: a branded “Let's get started” screen with an illustration of a person beside a lock, calm fields and one Continue button." caption="Login screen before and after. Smart validation was one of the main updates." /%}

{% result kind="partners" figures="24 of 31::rated 4 or 5|4.1/5::average" note="Post-launch partner survey, no baseline for the old version" %}
Most partners found getting started easy.
{% /result %}

{% figure src="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-flow-1-result.png" size="text" alt="Survey bar chart for installation and onboarding: 39% gave 5, 39% gave 4, 16% gave 3, 3% gave 2 and 3% gave 1. 31 responses, average 4.1." caption="Onboarding satisfaction after redesign (31 responses, avg 4.1/5)" /%}

## Alerts

**The main screen had all the information, but it was hard to read and scan**

The list showed a lot at once, with little spacing and inconsistent typography, so location and distance were hard to find.

{% pair %}
{% figure src="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-old-alerts-screen.jpg" frame="before" size="half" alt="The old alerts list, annotated: inconsistent typography, low spacing between items, and location, distance and time not prioritised." caption="Before: Alerts screen before redesign, showing density and unclear hierarchy" /%}
{% figure src="/media/projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-alerts-screen-redesign.mp4" frame="after" size="half" alt="The new alerts list: search at the top, alerts as spaced cards with location and distance first, and a persistent menu with a map button." caption="After: Improved alerts experience with clearer hierarchy and in-app guidance" /%}
{% /pair %}

{% runin text="Friction" /%} My first reaction to the old alerts page was "where should I be looking?". It showed eight alerts at once, with titles of 12 to 16 words squeezed into a tiny column, while tiny dates and distances competed for attention. It felt like a barrier to scanning and exploring, and there was no way to search by place, only a settings icon for picking regions. The new design added a search bar, a filter and a list/map toggle, inspired by map-and-listings layouts like Airbnb's, which I validated in a session with country office colleagues: the style was familiar and most welcomed it.

The goal was to improve scanning first, then support filtering (time and location) and help users distinguish new alerts. In-app guidance supports new users. Search sits at the top, filters are simplified, and a persistent menu gives clearer access to the map.

{% result kind="testing" figures="10 of 10::completed the location filter · 22 s · ease 9.3/10|13 of 13::completed the date filter · 19 s · ease 8.2/10" note="Maze tests with country office colleagues" %}
Testers filtered alerts by location and date without help.
{% /result %}

In the first test of the full flow (find explosions in one city over the last 8 hours), 12 of 13 testers completed it, but only 11 took the expected path, it took 119 seconds on average, and ease was 6.9/10 (usability score 66/100, the weakest of the list tests). One tester didn't realise the save icon was the thing to tap, another couldn't open the filters when the city wasn't in the first alerts shown.

{% figure src="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-flow-2-ut-test-01.jpg" alt="Maze results for filtering alerts by location: the task scenario next to the screens testers went through, with a 100% success rate." caption="Task-based usability test scenario and results for filtering alerts by location" /%}

## Alert detail

**I am in an alert. What information will help me the most?**

Interviews with country office colleagues showed that incident and location details are the first things people look for, followed by updates and advice. The former design contradicted that order, which was the main focus of the redesign.

{% figure src="/media/projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-alert-details-comparison.mp4" frame="compare" alt="Alert detail before: a map and a long block of text with the incident type buried. After: the title, place and time first, then full location, incident type, latest update and latest advice." caption="Alert detail screen before & after showing the differences in prioritisation of interests" /%}

{% runin text="Friction" /%} Alerts and their updates looked the same in the list, so a tester couldn't tell what had changed, and others asked for fuller location detail (country, province, territory) in the list and notification.

{% runin text="What I did" /%} Updates now carry their own marker, and the list and notifications show fuller location detail.

## Usability vs security

**Keeping alerts safe made the app harder to use**

Alerts are sensitive, so what people can copy or see was a long conversation with country office colleagues, and so was keeping the app discreet if someone looks at the phone. Finding the middle ground was tricky. It isn't fully settled, because the solutions to security concerns kept creating usability problems somewhere else: no copying or screenshots, several layers to get into the app, no Face ID. Partners named the copy and screenshot block as their biggest frustration.

## Maps

**Seeing incidents on a map changes everything**

The map helps users visualise incidents, explore locations and define areas of interest, whether they are on the move or monitoring remotely. In the former design, users could only see the location of a single alert, and a mode's settings sat behind a settings icon in another window. Settings for a feature belong inside that feature, so each map mode now holds its own.

{% figure src="/media/projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-map-former-design-assessment.mp4" frame="before" alt="The former map: it opens on one alert's pin, with no way to browse other alerts by place." caption="Before: In the former design, users could only view the location of a single alert on the map. Exploring alerts geographically, on the other hand, wasn’t straightforward." /%}

The first version I tested guided people with small pop-ups walking through the tools in each mode, without showing how they work. The results below are from that version.

### I'm planning a route across several checkpoints. What's happening along the way?

I introduced Explore mode, so users can move freely across the map and discover alerts visually.

{% figure src="/media/projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-map-explore.mp4" frame="after" alt="Explore mode: panning the map shows alert clusters, and tapping one opens the alert." caption="After: We introduced the Explore mode, allowing users to freely navigate the map and visually discover alerts." /%}

{% runin text="Friction" /%} Scrolling the alert detail also moved the map, and alerts without a location never reached the map, so list and map counts differed (one tester saw 10 in the list and 5 to 6 on the map). Fixing both cleared up a big source of confusion.

{% result kind="testing" figures="8 of 8::completed|33 s::on average|8.6/10::ease" note="Maze test with country office colleagues" %}
Testers found the map from the alerts list and explored it freely, with no drop-offs.
{% /result %}

### I'm not in the area I want to monitor. Can I still stay informed?

I reworked Proximity mode so users can monitor a radius around their current location or a custom pin.

{% figure src="/media/projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-map-proximity.mp4" frame="after" alt="Proximity mode: a circle around the user's position or a dropped pin, with a radius control, lists the alerts inside it." caption="After: I reworked the Proximity mode, allowing users to monitor alerts within a radius based on either their current location or a custom location dropped on the map." /%}

{% runin text="Friction" /%} In the first version you could set the radius in two places: on the map, and in the filter menu with a distance slider, a bit like dating apps do. In testing most people reached the map through the Map button on the alerts list and set their proximity there, so I removed the slider from the filter menu and kept one place to set a radius. The trickier part was that some testers didn't get the difference between proximity around their live location and around a custom location, or how to use either. That came down to a single pop-up introducing the whole feature: "Use this feature to know what's happening around you and/or while on the move. You can also set a remote location."

{% result kind="testing" figures="6 of 6::completed|53 s::on average|6.8/10::ease" note="Maze test with country office colleagues" %}
Testers set a radius around a place they weren't in, but the single pop-up left live and custom location unclear.
{% /result %}

I sat down with the engineers and a few colleagues from the information team at HQ to work up ideas, and two stood out:

**A doc or FAQ.** Pros: room to explain each feature and when it helps most, and it doesn't get in anyone's way on a first visit. Cons: a lot of extra steps to learn one thing, and on a phone you can't keep the doc and the map side by side like on a desktop.

**Full-screen animated tutorials.** Pros: quick and on demand, shown the first time someone opens a mode and again from Settings, so people see what it's for and how it works. Cons: three steps was the shortest I could get it, which might feel long for some.

We took the risk with the tutorials, since text and animation together get the point across faster than text alone.

{% figure src="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-map-modes-tutorial.jpg" alt="The map opens, then a full-screen tutorial introduces each mode in turn: Explore, Proximity and Geofence, each with a short line on what it is for, Next and Skip." caption="Full-screen tutorials introduce each map mode the first time the map opens." /%}

{% figure src="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-proximity-tutorial.jpg" frame="after" alt="The three-step Proximity tutorial: see what is around you, see what is around a custom location, and select the radius with a slider; then the results view, a radius on the map with the alerts inside it." caption="After: The three-step Proximity tutorial, then the results view." /%}

{% result kind="interviews" note="Interviews with 5 country office colleagues who hadn't seen the app before, updated design and prototype" %}
The tutorial cleared up the difference between live and custom location, and colleagues were more satisfied with the feature.
{% /result %}

### I need to track a very specific area. How do I define it without overthinking it?

Geofence was built for people comfortable with maps, such as security and GIS staff, not for every partner. Even so, it was the hardest feature to learn.

**Where I pushed back.** Colleagues in the country offices told me it takes some tech savviness, and even they didn't feel natural using it. Drawing an area also takes precision, which is far easier on a desktop than with a finger on a phone. The idea was to put the attention where it was needed the most, but the foundation was already there, an early feature called Polygon, so Geofence stayed and the job became making it usable. In that first version there were no instructions at all, just a map, and you were supposed to know you had to tap it to draw.

{% runin text="Friction" /%} In the prototype test, 5 of 8 testers completed the task, but none followed the intended path, and misclicks peaked while placing points (usability score 9/100, ease 6.4/10). People don't read: a pop-up explaining a feature like this doesn't work, they need to see it done step by step.

{% runin text="What I did" /%} Two testers asked for a video. Video is heavy on weak connections and easy to skip, so Geofence got the same kind of animated three-step tutorial (zoom, drop pins, confirm). It appears the first time someone opens Geofence and doesn't return once they've confirmed an area.

{% figure src="/media/projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-map-geofence.mp4" frame="after" alt="Geofence with the three-step guide: zoom in, tap to drop pins around an area, confirm, and the alerts inside the shape appear." caption="After: We simplified Geofence (formerly Polygon), allowing users to define areas directly by tapping on the map, supported by clear in-app guidance." /%}

{% result kind="interviews" note="Same 5 interviews; lowest-ranked feature in the post-launch partner survey (9 of 30), as expected for a specialist tool" %}
Colleagues understood Geofence, but saw it as relevant only to those who work actively with maps.
{% /result %}

Next idea (from a GIS officer): draw a line and set a buffer around a road or river.

Looking at screen views across releases, Explore takes most of the map traffic, with Proximity and Geofence well behind. That matches what partners ranked as most important, and it fits Geofence being a tool for a handful of specialists.

## After the first release

Interviews and working sessions with country office colleagues shaped what came next:

- Font size setting, a dark theme, and the app in French, English and Ukrainian (full Ukrainian since August 2023).
- Notifications limited to your regions or to a saved map search. People do open them: over 100,000 times from notifications in three years.
- An update marker and fuller location detail in the list and notifications.
- Unseen alerts pointed out, a Recent Alerts block, and a restyled date picker with quick options.
- Animated tutorials in every map mode.

The Ukraine country office was one of the offices that told us the most about internet coverage, which kept improving even through the war, compared with other countries in Africa and Asia.

{% figure src="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-new-alerts.jpg" frame="after" alt="Two alerts lists: the plain list, and the same list with a Recent Alerts block at the top holding the alerts not seen yet." caption="After: New alerts pointed out in the alerts list, with the Recent Alerts block on top." /%}

When the prototype went to colleagues, a few asked whether they could also post from the app. The prototype had shown that advanced features like map modes work on a phone without a big screen, which opened the door: senders need a map to drop exact pins, which we had assumed needed a computer. Field monitors often get information while busy (at a wedding, say) and then have to get to a laptop to post it, so posting from the phone solves a real problem. It needed management sign-off, which we got as it benefits several teams. I designed the first round in early 2025, and it was released in September 2025 together with login through an INSO account: a simple form where staff type the alert, set the location with coordinates or a pin on the map and choose which distribution lists (programmes) it goes to, plus updates to an existing alert, which arrive as an "Alert update: …" notification. Alerts sent this way show an "INSO ALERT" label, and staff get a megaphone button on the alerts screen. A later iteration added offline composing: the alert is saved on the phone and submitted as soon as the network is back.

{% figure src="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-compose.jpg" frame="after" alt="Composing an alert: the alerts list with an INSO ALERT label and a megaphone button, the empty form, the filled form with location and distribution lists, and the sent confirmation." caption="After: Composing an INSO ALERT from the phone, from the megaphone on the alerts list to the confirmation. Alert text, place names, the map and list sizes are blurred." /%}

---

## Outcome

**Outcome and takeaways**

**How the app is used today.** The launch month brought about 1,600 users, then a smaller base settled in: around 800 a month through 2024, and 450 to 630 through 2026. About 8 in 10 of them are returning users, so most people who use it keep coming back.

{% usagechart caption="Monthly active and returning users since launch, rounded. Test and visitor traffic left out." /%}

**What I can't say.** Nothing was measured before the redesign, so I can't say whether it made the app better, only that it's used and that people keep coming back.

{% result kind="partners" figures="23 of 29::rated ease 4 or 5 (79%)|4.1/5::average|24 of 31::for installation and onboarding" note="Post-launch partner survey" %}
Partners found the app easy to use.
{% /result %}

The weakest score was whether the app helped them reach their goal: 21 of 31 rated it 4 or 5 (average 3.7/5), and a few admitted they hadn't used it fully yet. In the same survey alert notifications came out as the most important feature (28 of 30), which confirms where to focus.

- **Design for the worst connection first.** Low bandwidth, stress and no time to learn shaped every decision, from step-by-step guides instead of video to a list that can be scanned in seconds.
- **Test with proxies, then check with the people themselves.** With no direct access before launch, I tested with country office colleagues and heard from partners afterwards.
- **Security and usability pull against each other.** Blocking copy and screenshots protects sensitive alerts, but partners named it their main frustration.

## Reflection

**What I'd do differently**

- Set baseline metrics before redesigning, so improvements can be shown.
- Run a formal retest of geofence.
- Set up tracking before a feature ships, so there's something to look at afterwards.

**About the composer.** The composer was released in September 2025, and I didn't test or track it afterwards. By then the team, me included, had been tasked with the Partners Portal, a one-stop platform where NGO workers get all of INSO's products and services, and that took our focus. A few months after the release I left INSO for another challenge.

**Still unsolved**

- Date filters that don't refresh, and frequent log-outs.
- Copy and screenshot restrictions that partners dislike.
- Offline use for reading alerts in regions without reliable internet. Two testers raised it, and it needs research before building.
- What partners asked for next: a LIVE indicator (22 of 30), map search (18 of 30) and region-based notifications (15 of 30).
